//? Title: Miller–Rabin primality
//? Subtitle: Actual strong rounds and deterministic unsigned64-bit decisions
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given an unsigned integer, decide whether its only positive divisors are one and
itself. We derive the strong probable-prime condition, show actual residues for
small primes and composites, expose a single-base pseudoprime, and retain the
original Carmichael examples and prime census. Twelve fixed bases suffice for
this API’s entire64-bit domain; one passing round alone does not prove
primality.
)

\toc

> Why repeated squares reveal composites

For odd n greater than2, factor n−1=d times2 to power s, with d odd. If n is
prime and a is not divisible by n, Fermat gives a to power(n−1) congruent to1.
Modulo a prime, the equation x squared=1 has only the two roots1 and−1: the
prime must divide one of the factors(x−1)(x+1). Therefore the sequence starting
at a to power d either starts at1, or encounters−1 before its first1. Failure
proves compositeness.

$$
a^d\equiv1\pmod n\quad\text{or}\quad a^{d2^r}\equiv-1\pmod n\ \text{for some }0\le r<s.
$$
\alttext(A passing strong round begins at one or reaches minus one among its first s residues.)

The converse fails for one base. A composite satisfying this condition is a
strong pseudoprime to that base. The code keeps the original scheduling: after
an unsuccessful initial residue it performs up to s−1 squares, stopping on−1; it
does not stop early on a later1. Every value below is observed inside the
canonical producer. Shared exact modular products use complete unsigned128-bit
intermediates.

> A small prime, from its actual states

For n=17 and base3, d=1 and s=4. The actual residues3,9,13,16 reach−1. The
arrows show actual scheduled squares, with the successful endpoint taken
directly from the trace. Passing is necessary for a prime; its meaning for a
composite depends on the chosen base.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/miller_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::miller_rabin_round_trace(17, 3);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("Actual d={}, s={}, pass={}.\n\n| r | actual residue |\n| ---: | ---: |\n",
               t.d, t.s, t.passed);
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < t.residues.size(); ++r) {
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", r, t.residues[r]);
        auto id = std::to_string(r);
        d.node(id, {5.0 * r, 0},
               std::format("r={}\nresidue {}", r, t.residues[r]));
        if (r)
            d.edge(std::to_string(r - 1), id);
    }
    d.save_svg("build/miller_tiny.svg");
}
```
