> Retain the original worked rounds and Carmichael controls

The original prime13 passes base2 with residues8,12. Composite15 fails with
residue8. Composite561 has d=35 and s=4; base2 produces263,166,67,1 without
encountering560. In particular67 is a nontrivial square root of1 modulo561,
impossible modulo a prime. Its Fermat residue is nevertheless1. The original1105
and1729 are also composite; Fermat passes shown here are numerical observations
for base2.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/miller_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| candidate | d | s | base2 strong pass | deterministic prime | base2 Fermat residue | " "actual strong residues |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::uint64_t n : {13ULL, 15ULL, 561ULL, 1105ULL, 1729ULL}) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::miller_rabin_round_trace(n, 2);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |", n, t.d, t.s, t.passed,
                   dm::algorithms::is_probable_prime(n),
                   dm::algorithms::mod_pow(2, n - 1, n));
        for (auto v : t.residues)
            std::print(" {}", v);
        std::print(" |\n");
        if (n == 561)
            for (std::size_t r = 0; r < t.residues.size(); ++r) {
                x.push_back(r);
        y.push_back(t.residues[r]);
            }
    }
    p.points(x, y, "actual base2 residues", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Original 561 round: no minus-one residue").x_label(
        "post-powering square index").y_label("residue modulo561").save_svg(
        "build/miller_original.svg");
}
```
