> Retain the census and define the limits

The original census enumerates2..2000 using the deterministic native function.
Its303 primes and cumulative counts describe this finite interval, not an
asymptotic proof about prime distribution. No external dataset is needed for
this integer decision problem.

Each strong round uses at most the bit width of d for powering plus s−1
post-powering squares. With a fixed number of bases, the driver counts O(log n)
fixed-word operations and uses constant storage. The trace adds O(log n)
records. These are not arbitrary-precision bit-complexity or timing guarantees.
Capture caps bound powering bit records plus all s potentially tested residues
before powering, even if the actual round exits earlier; default128, zero
disables. Below2 fails, even n passes only for2, and a base multiple of odd n
passes vacuously without powering. These conventions are API behavior, not
useful primality evidence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/miller_census.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::size_t count = 0;
    for (std::uint64_t n = 2; n <= 2000; ++n) {
        count += dm::algorithms::is_probable_prime(n);
        x.push_back(n);
        y.push_back(count);
    }
    std::print("Primes in2..2000: {}.\n\n| candidate | base | single-round pass | actual residues |\n| ---: " "| ---: | --- | ---: |\n",
               count);
    for (auto[n, a] : std::vector<std::pair<std::uint64_t, std::uint64_t>>{
         {0, 2}, {1, 2}, {2, 0}, {4, 2}, {9, 18}, {18446744073709551557ULL, 2}
     }) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::miller_rabin_round_trace(n, a);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a, t.passed, t.residues.size());
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "native cumulative primes").title("Original census: deterministic primes through2000").x_label("upper endpoint").y_label("cumulative prime count").save_svg("build/miller_census.svg");
}
```
